    - target: {fileID: 2136312619905546357, guid: 7ffcca573e1296d47a9191342db0701b,
        type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_text
      value: 'Usually, objects are rendered by using a bunch of triangle faces. However
        this is not the only way of doing so.


        We start with a mathematicall
        description how far the surface of an object is away from any point. These
        are called SDF or Sined Distance Functions. Positive values are outside the
        shape, negative ones inside.


        For each pixel, we can then start
        at the camera position and walk in the direction of the pixel. To figure
        out how far we are able to safely walk without hitting anything, we use the
        SDF.Since the function is non-directional, we need to keep doing this.


        Unless
        the face we will eventually hit is perpendicular to our direction, we will
        never actually hit it. Therfore, a threshold is a useful cutoff.'
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
