      propertyPath: m_text
      value: 'Modeling tiny surface details and displaying them as a mesh would be
        extremely costly. Therefore, normal maps are used, which represent the offset
        between the model surface and the surface that should be displayed. This
        can then be used in the light calculation.


        Normal maps are in tangent
        space, which means they are projected on a tangential plane that touches
        the surface at the sampling point. The color channels are remapped from 0...1
        to -1...1.  Red represents the tangent direction, green the bitangent direction
        and blue the normal direction. OpenGL (and Unity) uses a right while DirectX
        uses a left handed coordinate system standard, which means the bitangent
        is sometimes inverted.


        Since they are normalized, only 2 channels
        are actually needed. Therefore, Unity uses an internal packing and unpacking
        method. To convert the normal maps to world space, the TBN (Tangent, Bitangent,
